Presidential elections were held in [[El Salvador]] on 3 February 2019, with Salvadorans electing the [[President of El Salvador|president]] and [[Vice President of El Salvador|vice president]] for a five-year term from 2019 to 2024.

The election resulted in victory for [[Nayib Bukele]] of the right-wing [[Grand Alliance for National Unity]] (GANA), who received 53%, defeating [[Carlos Calleja]] of the right-wing [[Nationalist Republican Alliance]] (ARENA), [[Hugo Martínez (politician)|Hugo Martínez]] of the left-wing [[Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front]] (FMLN) and Josué Alvarado of the centrist [[Vamos (El Salvador)|Vamos]] party.<ref name="AQ">{{cite news|first1=Emilie|last1=Sweigart|title=El Salvador: Meet the Candidates in Latin America's First Election of 2019|url=https://www.americasquarterly.org/content/el-salvador-meet-candidates-latin-americas-first-election-2019|work=[[Americas Quarterly]]|date=30 January 2019|access-date=3 February 2019|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190203130045/https://www.americasquarterly.org/content/el-salvador-meet-candidates-latin-americas-first-election-2019 |archive-date=3 February 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> With his victory, Bukele became the first president since [[José Napoleón Duarte]] (1984–1989) to not be a member of either ARENA or the FMLN, which had controlled the presidency in a [[two-party system]] from 1989 to 2019.

Prior to the elections, Bukele held a lead against Calleja, Martínez and Alvarado in virtually every poll conducted between July 2018 and January 2019. A [[Two-round system|second round]] in March was rendered unnecessary as Bukele won an outright majority; Bukele won a plurality in all of the country's [[Departments of El Salvador|fourteen departments]], winning an outright majority in eight of them.<ref>{{cite news |title=Así avanza el conteo de votos en las elecciones presidenciales de El Salvador |url=https://www.elsalvador.com/noticias/nacional/564333/cierran-los-centros-de-votacion-y-comienza-el-conteo-de-votos-en-las-elecciones-presidenciales-de-el-salvador/ |access-date=4 February 2019 |publisher=elsalvador.com |date=3 February 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=El Salvador: anti-corruption candidate Nayib Bukele wins presidential election |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2019/feb/04/el-salvador-anti-corruption-candidate-nayib-bukele-wins-presidential-election |access-date=4 February 2019 |newspaper=The Guardian |date=4 February 2019}}</ref> Bukele was inaugurated on 1 June 2019.<ref name="Bin">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-48458077|title=Nayib Bukele: El Salvador's Incoming Leader Promises 'New Era'|language=en|date=1 June 2019|access-date=2 April 2023|work=[[BBC]]|first1=Blaire|last1=Toedte}}</ref>

== Background ==

=== Presidency of Salvador Sánchez Cerén ===

{{see|2014 Salvadoran presidential election|2015 Salvadoran legislative election|2018 Salvadoran legislative election}}

[[File:Acto oficial Traspaso de mando Presidencial 18.jpg|thumb|left|220px|[[Salvador Sánchez Cerén]] at his inauguration in 2014.]]
[[Salvador Sánchez Cerén]], the then [[Vice President of El Salvador|vice president of El Salvador]], won the [[2014 Salvadoran presidential election|2014 presidential election]] by a narrow margin. As a member of the left-wing [[Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front]] (FMLN), he defeated [[Norman Quijano]], the then [[mayor of San Salvador]] of the right-wing [[Nationalist Republican Alliance]], in that election's second round, winning 50.11 percent of the vote by a margin of 6,364 votes.<ref name="2014 election">{{cite web|author1=Supreme Electoral Court|author1-link=Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memoria-de-elecciones/memoria-elecciones-2014.pdf|date=2014|access-date=9 January 2023|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|language=es|title=Memorial Especial – Elecciones 2014|trans-title=Special Memory – 2014 Elections|page=144}}</ref> Sánchez Cerén was inaugurated on 1 June 2014, succeeding fellow FMLN President [[Mauricio Funes]]. He was the first former guerrilla fighter from the [[Salvadoran Civil War]] to be elected president.<ref name="SSCin">{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2014/6/2/ex-rebel-sworn-in-as-el-salvador-president|title=Ex-rebel Sworn in as El Salvador President|language=en|work=[[Al Jazeera English|Al Jazeera]]|date=2 June 2014|access-date=2 April 2023}}</ref>

Although in control of the presidency, the FMLN did not have a majority of the [[Legislative Assembly of El Salvador|Legislative Assembly]], with power being divided between it, ARENA, and various other political parties. In the [[2015 Salvadoran legislative election|2015 legislative election]], the FMLN won 31 seats and ARENA won 35 seats, with the remaining 18 seats being controlled by other parties.<ref name="2015 election">{{cite web|author1=Supreme Electoral Court|author1-link=Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memorias-de-elecciones/memoria-elecciones-2015.pdf|date=2018|access-date=2 April 2023|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|language=es|title=Memorial Especial – Elecciones 2015|trans-title=Special Memory – 2015 Elections|page=133}}</ref> In the succeeding [[2018 Salvadoran legislative election|2018 legislative election]], the FMLN fell to 23 seats while ARENA increased to 38 seats, with the remaining 23 seats being controlled by other parties.<ref name="2018 election">{{cite web|author1=Supreme Electoral Court|author1-link=Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|url=https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memoria-de-elecciones/memoria-elecciones-2018.pdf|date=2018|access-date=2 April 2023|work=[[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]]|language=es|title=Memorial Especial – Elecciones 2018|trans-title=Special Memory – 2018 Elections|page=104|archive-date=22 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221222073607/https://www.tse.gob.sv/documentos/memoria-de-elecciones/memoria-elecciones-2018.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref>
== Electoral system ==

=== Electoral procedure ===

In October 2017, the [[Supreme Electoral Court (El Salvador)|Supreme Electoral Court]] (TSE) scheduled [[El Salvador]]'s upcoming 2019 presidential election to occur on 3 February 2019, with a possible [[Two-round system|second round]] being scheduled for 10 March 2019. In the election, Salvadorans would elect the country's [[President of El Salvador|president]] and [[Vice President of El Salvador|vice president]] to serve a five-year term from 2019 to 2024.<ref name="Date">{{cite web|url=http://elmundo.sv/tse-define-fecha-para-eleccion-presidencial-2019/|title=TSE Define Fecha para Elección Presidencial 2019|trans-title=TSE Defines Date for the 2019 Presidential Election|language=es|date=16 October 2017|access-date=9 January 2019|work=[[El Mundo (El Salvador)|El Mundo]]|first1=Edgardo|last1=Rivera|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190109012121/https://elmundo.sv/tse-define-fecha-para-eleccion-presidencial-2019/|archive-date=9 January 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref>

A presidential candidate needed to win an [[Majority|absolute majority]] (50%{{Nbsp}}+{{Nbsp}}1) to be declared the winner of the election. If no candidate received an absolute majority, a second between the two candidates with the most valid votes would have occurred. All presidential and vice presidential candidates must have been at least 30 years old and be Salvadoran citizens by birth.<ref name="constitution">{{cite web|url=http://confinder.richmond.edu/admin/docs/ElSalvador1983English.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150103200933/http://confinder.richmond.edu/admin/docs/ElSalvador1983English.pdf|date=15 December 1983|archive-date=3 January 2015|access-date=12 November 2021|title=Constitution of El Salvador|language=en|website=Richmond.edu|pages=Articles 78–80}}</ref><ref name="Nohlen">{{cite book|last1=Krennerich|first1=Michael|chapter=El Salvador|date=2005|editor-last1=Nohlen|editor-first1=Dieter|editor1-link=Dieter Nohlen|title=Elections in the Americas A Data Handbook Volume 1: North America, Central America, and the Caribbean|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=-xZREAAAQBAJ|language=en|location=[[Oxford]], United Kingdom|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|pages=270–299|isbn=9780191557934|oclc=58051010|access-date=9 January 2023}}</ref>{{Rp|272–274}}

